course: religion-ethics
lesson: 54
title: Life is precious: wonder and gratitude
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Each person gets one life on this earth, and nobody else can live it for them; that is a big part of why every life, yours and everyone else's, is precious.
explain: Picture Luca's class watching a film of a chick pecking its way out of an egg with a tiny tooth on its beak that nobody taught it to use; the whole class goes «awww», and that feeling is wonder.
explain: Giving thanks, even for pizza, a dog called Biscuit or a dry sports day, helps people notice what is good; Christians thank God, Muslims say «Alhamdulillah», Jews say blessings called brachot, and non-religious people may keep a list of things they are grateful for.
explain: Ordinary days with food, safety and family are something many people in the world do not have, so a «boring» day is also a lucky one, though it is still fine to feel bored or disappointed.
explain: Thankfulness leads to kindness: we do not hurt living things for fun, and at harvest festival churches fill up with tins and bread to share with people who do not have enough.
fact: Each person has one life on this earth; nobody else can live it for them.
fact: A chick grows in its egg for about three weeks and cracks the shell using a small «egg tooth» on its beak.
fact: Studies suggest that people who often give thanks tend to feel happier.
fact: Christians say grace before meals and thank God for big and small things; many Jews and Muslims also give thanks at meals.
fact: Muslims say «Alhamdulillah», which means «praise be to God», when good things happen; Jews say blessings called brachot; Christians may say «Thanks be to God».
fact: A snail is a living creature; killing animals for fun is unkind, and Buddhists and Jains try not to harm any living thing at all.
fact: Christians believe each person is made «in the image of God»; humanists also teach that every human life has great worth.
fact: Some families celebrate the day someone recovered from a serious illness, to say «we are so glad you are still here».
fact: Harvest festival thanks God for food; churches collect food for food banks, which give food to people who do not have enough.
fact: Other faiths have harvest festivals too, such as the Jewish Sukkot and the Hindu Pongal.
fact: Big feelings can come out as tears, both sad and happy; at a baptism the church thanks God for a child's life.
fact: The psalm says «This is the day the Lord has made; let us rejoice and be glad in it».
exercise: Daisy thanks God for pizza, her dog and a dry sports day. Does thanks for small things count? | Yes, noticing small good things is real thankfulness | No, only big things like health count | No, God only hears thanks that are said out loud in church | Yes, but only if you say it ten times
exercise: Luca's class watches a chick hatch. How does it get out of the egg? | It cracks the shell with a tiny tooth on its beak | Its mother pecks and breaks the shell open from outside | The egg melts when the chick is ready | A farmer cuts the shell open each time
exercise: Tyler steps on a snail to hear the crunch. What is wrong with that? | It kills a living creature just for fun | Nothing, because snails are not really alive at all | It might damage the bottom of his new shoe | Snails belong to the council, so it is stealing
exercise: Priya's family has cake every year on the day her mum came home from hospital. Why? | To celebrate that she got better and is still with them | Because the hospital asked them to | To make the doctors jealous | Because cake is good medicine
exercise: Max says his life is boring and his grandad, who lived through a war, goes quiet. Why? | Safe, ordinary days are something many people never had | Grandad agrees that Max's life is terrible | Grandad has fallen asleep at the table | Grandad wants Max to join an army
exercise: Ella says life is precious «because you only get one». What does she mean? | Nobody else can live your life, and you get one on this earth | People get a brand new life to start all over again every birthday | Life is precious only for children | Life is precious because it costs money
exercise: Ben's mum says, «We're all healthy and together. That's the holiday.» What is true? | Health and family matter more than trips, though disappointment is fine | Holidays are bad for children | Ben is wrong to want anything ever | Being together is the same as going to Spain
exercise: Holly's church fills with tins and bread at harvest festival. Why give food away? | To thank God for food and share with people who are hungry | To get rid of food that has gone off | Because the church sells it on Monday | Because tins are far too heavy to keep in the cupboard at home
exercise: Mohammed's family says «Alhamdulillah». What does it mean? | Praise be to God | Good morning to you | Let us eat now | Goodbye and good luck
exercise: Jamie's mum cried happy tears at his baptism video. Why? | Big feelings of love can come out as tears | She was secretly sad that he had been born at all | Baptism water got in her eyes | Grown-ups cry at every video they watch
exercise: What do Christians mean by saying people are made «in the image of God»? | Every person has great worth | People look exactly like God | Only Christians are worth anything | People should paint pictures of God
exercise: What do Buddhists and Jains try to do? | Not harm any living thing at all | Eat only snails and insects, never plants | Kill pests as fast as they possibly can | Keep as many pets at home as they can
exercise: Which of these is a Jewish harvest festival? | Sukkot | Diwali | Ramadan | Pentecost
exercise: What do people who give thanks often tend to feel, according to studies? | Happier | More tired | More jealous | Nothing different at all
exercise: What is grace before meals? | A short prayer thanking God for food | A little dance that is done before dinner | A rule that you must always eat slowly | A name for the person who cooks the meal
test: Daisy's brother, a proper Scouser, says, «God caring about pizza? Don't be soft, la.» («Soft» means «silly».) What is the fair answer? | Thankfulness for little things is still real thankfulness | He is right, small thanks don't count | God only cares about pizza on Sundays | Thanks must always be for something huge, like a new house or a car
test: Tyler laughs, «It's just a snail innit, who cares.» What is the best reply? | It's alive and wants to live; don't hurt living things for fun | Fine, snails can't feel anything at all | Fine, as long as nobody sees you | Snails are pests in the garden, so crunching them is actually kind
test: A teen cousin says, «My life's so mid, nothing ever happens, I'm literally dead.» («Mid» means «average, dull»; «I'm dead» means «I'm so bored».) What might her grandad say? | «Boring is lovely, I'd have given anything for boring» | «You're right, safe days are worthless» | «You should go and find a war» | «Being bored means you're ill»
test: What would happen if nobody ever said thank you for anything? | People might notice less of what is good and feel less glad | Everyone would be much happier | Food would stop growing on farms | Nothing would ever go wrong again
test: Ben moans, «That's such a mum thing to say.» His mum said health and family are the real holiday. Who is right? | It does sound like a mum thing, and it is also true | Ben is right, it is just nonsense that mums say to children | Mum is wrong, holidays matter most | Neither, because nobody can be grateful
test: A New Zealand mate says, «Sweet as, harvest heaps of tins for the food bank, chur!» («Sweet as» means «great»; «chur» means «thanks, cheers».) What is he pleased about? | Food being collected to share with hungry people | The church selling tins for profit | The food bank closing down for good and giving everything away | Tins being thrown away after harvest
test: Holly says to Mohammed, «Alhamdulillah sounds just like our "thanks be to God".» Is she right? | Yes, different words carry the same thankful heart | No, Muslims never give thanks | No, it means «good luck» | Yes, but only Christians are ever allowed to say it out loud
test: A Gen Alpha kid says the hatching chick video is «bussin', it ate!» («Bussin'» and «it ate» mean «really amazing».) What feeling is he showing? | Wonder at how a chick hatches | Hunger for a big omelette for tea | Boredom with all nature videos | Fear of chickens and their beaks
test: Priya's mate says, «A party for leaving hospital? Weird.» Which answer explains it best? | The family is glad she is still here after coming so close | Leaving hospital is always a sort of holiday for every family | They were celebrating the hospital food | It is a rule that families must party
test: A boy from Glasgow says, «Ma mammy greeted at ma christening video.» («Greeted» means «cried».) Why might she cry happily? | She felt so much love for her child | She was disappointed by the video and how it looked | She had a bad cold and a runny nose that day | Christening videos are always sad to watch
test: «If you only get one life, why do people waste it?» asks Ella. Which is the wisest answer? | Everyone wastes time sometimes, but we can choose to spend our days well | Nobody ever wastes any time | Life is so short that nothing matters | People get spare lives, so they can waste one
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of the Arabic expression «Alhamdulillah»? | An utterance of praise to God upon the occurrence of good fortune | A formal greeting exchanged at the commencement of the day, as at dawn | A declaration of departure at the conclusion of a meeting | A request for nourishment prior to a meal
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the harvest festival as observed in Christian churches. | The giving of thanks for food and the sharing of it with those in want | The sale of agricultural produce for the benefit of the clergy | The disposal of foodstuffs which have passed their date of use | The commemoration of the founding of the parish
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principles of Buddhism and Jainism in respect of living creatures. | The avoidance of harm to any living being | The destruction of creatures deemed to be pests | The keeping of creatures for entertainment | Indifference to the suffering of small creatures
exam: Ascertain the means by which a chick effects its emergence from the egg. | By means of a small egg tooth situated upon its beak | By means of assistance from the parent bird externally | By means of the spontaneous dissolution of the shell | By means of the intervention of the farmer
